  "generated_text": "1. Calculate the total number of possible license plates:\n   - First letter: 5 options\n   - Second letter: 3 options\n   - Third letter: 4 options\n   - Total current license plates: \\( 5 \\times 3 \\times 4 = 60 \\)\n\n2. Determine the maximum number of additional license plates that can be made by adding two new letters:\n   - If both new letters are added to one set, the number of options for that set increases by 2.\n   - If one new letter is added to one set and one to another set, the number of options for each set increases by 1.\n\n3. Calculate the maximum number of additional license plates:\n   - If both new letters are added to one set, the number of options for that set increases by 2.\n   - If one new letter is added to one set and one to another set, the number of options for each set increases by 1.\n   - Total additional license plates: \\( 2 + 2 = 4 \\)\n\nTherefore, the largest possible number of ADDITIONAL license plates that can be made by adding two new letters is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[4, '4']",
    "gold_answer": "Before new letters were added, five different letters could have been chosen for the first position, three for the second, and four for the third. This means that $5\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=60$ plates could have been made.\n\nIf two letters are added to the second set, then $5\\cdot 5\\cdot 4=100$ plates can be made. If one letter is added to each of the second and third sets, then $5\\cdot 4\\cdot 5=100$ plates can be made. None of the other four ways to place the two letters will create as many plates. So, $100-60=\\boxed{40}$ ADDITIONAL plates can be made.\n\nNote: Optimum results can usually be obtained in such problems by making the factors as nearly equal as possible.",
    "is_correct": false
  },
